{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00153 - MONDO_1068",
  "verbalization": "Ergocalciferol, also known as vitamin D3, is implicated in the pathogenesis of osteomalacia through a series of biochemical pathways that involve the regulation of calcium homeostasis. The evidence suggests that Ergocalciferol is associated with Rickets (disease) via a chain of indications, with Rickets leading to Osteomalacia (disease) through the involvement of dihydrotachysterol, calcifediol, or cholecalciferol. These compounds are all forms of vitamin D, and their roles in the disease are likely mediated through their ability to activate vitamin D receptors, which are essential for the absorption of calcium and phosphorus in the intestines. The presence of multiple indirect pathways linking Ergocalciferol to Osteomalacia underscores the importance of vitamin D in maintaining bone health and suggests that the disease may result from impaired vitamin D metabolism or utilization. The cumulative evidence supports a mechanistic link between Ergocalciferol and Osteomalacia, particularly through its role in the regulation of vitamin D-dependent processes that are critical for bone mineralization.",
